#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/id #uuid "cbabcd64-6a33-552d-9f2f-704a9df44d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10906" :question/problem "The set $\\{ \\mathbf{a_1},\\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n![](images/2f/2fc772925dd6b793bdcb3a1eca96ea7e7f5fed17425f3500638340e047170c60.png)\n\n![](images/f7/f73258610652de9a656f3c4ed202241c58c98f18391b1393dabe62d92d9bef7d.png)" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n![](images/21/210a5c50467861cdfe48b46f2098212bbf387270b420a05394e318a0e0b48c74.png)\nTo see why, notice that \n![](images/91/91a29b57d32ed8c8e702f2988f7714ffc343ed6b4707b86b49b708ffad5c8bc8.png)\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1  +x_2  \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=\\dfrac 1 3$ and $x_2=-1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "7b28b42f-0ac4-543e-baeb-51534aaad480" :question/math-academy-id "e-10907" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](images/e9/e9d8c1ab6f31d1ca580343f16d10eb55b6aae24db679129540a30b552cedaf19.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0.$\n\n- The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/id #uuid "3843dc13-0908-5d4c-adfe-836a928a1410" :question/math-academy-id "e-10908" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](images/8d/8d88cae0e40e7a377314e359086dc45fa0460c17ed55fe379cc15537886b2623.png)\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2,$ and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ while $x_3$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f3b240-c702-580d-9cdf-43123253f773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396587} {:answer/id #uuid "574dea89-ee9d-5556-a72b-c257c732699e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396588} {:answer/id #uuid "640a91d9-57f7-5e99-bc15-29e1456e8af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396589} {:answer/id #uuid "2044901c-e971-534a-af35-fba5ec803324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396590} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf8a5fb-65f3-58d7-a288-3f0a87f9681a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2044901c-e971-534a-af35-fba5ec803324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396590} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01010bc-5e11-583a-98fc-7b20994c0fc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "faa7ddb4-5b88-5e56-a53e-35fdb9d2ef93" :question/math-academy-id "q-105291" :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a7491d-2ce9-57b5-afc5-475cffb86e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238202} {:answer/id #uuid "81792f0f-eb2e-5c8b-9e65-a32b2c8a647e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239178} {:answer/id #uuid "84b00443-b844-5946-b246-e24069d4f374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239208} {:answer/id #uuid "c37a4610-6917-5cd7-8438-a4eaaee5a60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239781} {:answer/id #uuid "d87befb5-db05-5e3b-b1bc-bbf4875296fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d87befb5-db05-5e3b-b1bc-bbf4875296fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239945} :answer-field/id #uuid "daefdae9-19ab-5066-96c6-64e5270f1794" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e5fd550-1965-59ea-b83f-2c578a1e0197" :question/math-academy-id "q-105296" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1564190f-a603-5a1b-99ee-d29458e3bdf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238231} {:answer/id #uuid "67430bf0-add9-5760-a0d2-5687fc3ea820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238953} {:answer/id #uuid "771afedc-1ab9-538b-9c8d-99514ab7bd87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239082} {:answer/id #uuid "a297bb83-555e-542b-b729-eb9040d9fd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239468} {:answer/id #uuid "edc71038-d293-56dc-bedb-b4c724d2c367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edc71038-d293-56dc-bedb-b4c724d2c367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240151} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1c4cc2-96e9-5425-b903-8dcaac1d1c80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "070a6728-701f-5a05-b9c9-03b5e1530505" :question/math-academy-id "q-105297" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=-2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6b3754-c8d5-5b37-8d18-ef83f5e9749c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238185} {:answer/id #uuid "27e14937-1c82-559e-a37e-1b482fa437f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238401} {:answer/id #uuid "366bc6b5-4bab-5ba8-8018-c4bc7223b115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238525} {:answer/id #uuid "69582467-05df-57a2-b597-530abbc3877d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238970} {:answer/id #uuid "93d981b6-c7f1-52e4-a22e-60dd625357db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27e14937-1c82-559e-a37e-1b482fa437f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238401} :answer-field/id #uuid "b17fe9cc-10a6-5ca0-8dc9-5ed726aaaab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c05cb83d-c779-50b1-a772-7d4551eea239" :question/math-academy-id "q-105308" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-16{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430d277a-8ae9-5f5c-b444-1e030f7d4b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238638} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ee60d9-cc92-5ae5-8f48-faec6aa345f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239628} {:answer/id #uuid "b53ef7a2-cefd-53e6-9d85-717e63e61041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239638} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d27227-465c-53c2-ba01-b2b6e324387d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239956} {:answer/id #uuid "f2066ce9-514d-5cb7-945c-0ead2cf3ec28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "430d277a-8ae9-5f5c-b444-1e030f7d4b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238638} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ca901c-c56f-5777-a1c4-6a7cf44555ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af32236d-520e-5b71-b7ec-9663a181498c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105310" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 15 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -7 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -7 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement III is false, while statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6dca7d-0ce4-5e8e-b9b2-3303fd96d94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117089} {:answer/id #uuid "d51a6c38-bbb9-53c8-9e8d-d08531e3e2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117090} {:answer/id #uuid "e90efca0-0287-5643-adc8-6e36ca3d9e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117091} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5fc17f-c8a6-5808-8a11-b0ae8b5d686d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117092} {:answer/id #uuid "669cbbfb-8d77-5f7b-a387-18e5ffbd5163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117093}] :answer-field/id #uuid "01b88459-f835-5654-8214-92ece439c385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117088}] :question/id #uuid "9ec28c6e-377f-591a-9037-3511702a70c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-155965" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $2a_{1} - a_{2} + a_{3} = 0$.\n2. $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = 0$ only if $x_{1} = x_{2} = x_{3} = 0$.\n3. The set ${a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$ is linearly independent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5806b72c-a0de-522d-9575-297c97f66e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273142} {:answer/id #uuid "82608251-0a92-5dd0-8eb5-d194076eb4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273505} {:answer/id #uuid "afbc140c-4487-5281-9e71-ad12441a9572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273906} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7c417e-8874-5c6d-81ad-7db977b43fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274028} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f9a778-7655-51b8-a6f6-a2b9c41e7f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afbc140c-4487-5281-9e71-ad12441a9572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273906} :answer-field/id #uuid "3579701a-9198-5e56-82f7-d569880f78f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62e09db0-cf5e-5972-a5d2-6baa771c45b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-155967" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d82b46-aff2-515f-9d9e-763c1d8d6d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272442} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae733d2-cc04-5192-bed0-23294bc49d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273728} {:answer/id #uuid "ac235c0f-3456-5dc9-bf15-4e714898bb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273872} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e7a6e4-13cc-528d-b440-977d47c6f5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274257} {:answer/id #uuid "f92b98ef-714d-56ae-84c7-20ba88faf358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae733d2-cc04-5192-bed0-23294bc49d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273728} :answer-field/id #uuid "3340ab30-5665-5c4b-ac26-0abb6f1cea04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97d72a17-2281-52f3-a3f9-d658f3c735d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-157224" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =-2[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-2+0 \\\\ 0+4+0 \\\\ 2-2+15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100da246-a4f5-543a-91d0-e6d3a1f36af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272530} {:answer/id #uuid "3f886435-adcb-5d14-a0ea-d8238504ef8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272932} {:answer/id #uuid "74151cc2-d101-54b9-b1d6-6503644e7ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273380} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9e7313-37e7-5fd3-8c10-525805d2da7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273622} {:answer/id #uuid "ee688365-d420-5470-af23-7bd64976045b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f886435-adcb-5d14-a0ea-d8238504ef8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272932} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8437634-a0b8-5fc4-8ac1-18de47b710d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40a86374-09c1-5fcd-a58c-a5f678fb3c1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-157228" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+5{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+5{a}_{3} & =-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+0+15 \\\\ -7-10+20 \\\\ 0+0+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fd0e5f-aec1-5e47-9884-6914dcdde436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272432} {:answer/id #uuid "53f1398f-c03c-5a8d-aa1c-eb0e6f8cff38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273102} {:answer/id #uuid "ac422526-fbb5-5674-b3b5-76631c070aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273873} {:answer/id #uuid "ceac787a-b79a-5630-828b-66afa1cf3251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274184} {:answer/id #uuid "fc49f684-7f45-59ee-80e0-cb6b1d9c3f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac422526-fbb5-5674-b3b5-76631c070aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273873} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df80ac5-b285-5b02-8dca-ab5fb0ce1e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2473ef72-1c36-57f9-bdd5-12c0b04c9229" :question/math-academy-id "q-157236" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3+4 \\\\ 5-6+1 \\\\ 2-12+10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172bfc45-43c6-5f9b-90f6-930b6748c07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315249} {:answer/id #uuid "30e01cba-42d8-51c3-9004-5c057629e1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315397} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe5d1ab-8253-5b89-a5c0-ca1372be3314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315485} {:answer/id #uuid "5f087a9c-6631-5e06-a1ea-ba0a59a17a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315654} {:answer/id #uuid "ae94241c-5c88-5581-8a78-e3b6112151f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fe5d1ab-8253-5b89-a5c0-ca1372be3314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315485} :answer-field/id #uuid "483bd6df-6359-5977-8496-d54fb13d179d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62d9b5f5-3d93-555f-bd13-2fa9aa2fe12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-34271" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24069f64-c580-5200-b5e0-1c7ef2ae7e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315321} {:answer/id #uuid "3a15928d-e29a-514a-aa9c-b81e928ccc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315451} {:answer/id #uuid "6b92d802-3085-528a-9c72-efc57cf5c2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315724} {:answer/id #uuid "800f5135-a705-5bc5-b182-cd0428d00e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315821} {:answer/id #uuid "93c3185c-8c40-5110-addf-07d04037df9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a15928d-e29a-514a-aa9c-b81e928ccc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315451} :answer-field/id #uuid "22bb0e27-d6d9-5966-9fb5-317681ddbc7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1120ac93-1146-5212-b323-41cae9ac4014" :question/math-academy-id "q-34276" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da42d2a-fc8c-5560-a05e-8ebb08251ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342746} {:answer/id #uuid "428056c7-8c15-5083-b495-0a5d2db7568d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342788} {:answer/id #uuid "b05bd9d9-23cf-5926-8e64-3aa606f0b446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343789} {:answer/id #uuid "f974dbb8-fa3b-57e5-b4a2-cbf533523461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344432} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6841f4-2ac2-55ac-8a6c-00752966eb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05bd9d9-23cf-5926-8e64-3aa606f0b446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343789} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c97eb9a-a992-5953-a80c-a71608c9beca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d48ab70-ab46-5142-a432-382f1feb7bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75659" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11972855-80a9-5cac-9e70-8a4fcbdccf24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342393} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfeba34-6da2-5961-afdc-9d9564e3b098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342608} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5b646e-db57-5a8d-9e82-9eb0862b3d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343756} {:answer/id #uuid "acba01cf-19c6-57a0-8cf2-d050702ea3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343758} {:answer/id #uuid "f9874691-41fb-52a1-b9c0-d3eb10d101ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5b646e-db57-5a8d-9e82-9eb0862b3d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343756} :answer-field/id #uuid "d156fe28-1c7f-5127-9119-2df01429f973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d177800-f6a6-59b3-bf6e-5adc772e358a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75660" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+13{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 13 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true."}]}
